"""English — details, virtues (32 questions)."""

D = {
"virtues_019": "'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 6094, in the Book of Good Manners. It does not stop at the two roads: a man keeps telling the truth until he is written with Allah as a siddiq, wholly truthful, and keeps lying until he is written with Allah as a liar. The Qur'an gives the same direction: \"Fear Allah and be with the truthful\" (9:119). A Muslim learns that each honest word is a step on a road with a known end.",
"virtues_020": "Abu Hurayra narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 33, placed in the Book of Faith under the heading of the signs of the hypocrite. The three signs all touch what a person says to others: his news, his promise, his trust. The Qur'an describes the hypocrites of Madinah the same way, swearing that Muhammad ﷺ was the Messenger of Allah while \"Allah testifies that the hypocrites are liars\" (63:1). A Muslim examines his own speech, promises and trusts against these three marks first.",
"virtues_021": "Abu Hurayra narrates the exchange in Sahih al-Bukhari 6116, in the chapter on guarding against anger. The man received no list of duties; he asked more than once and heard the same three words each time, so the whole advice fitted one sentence. The Qur'an counts among the people of Paradise \"those who restrain anger and pardon people\" (3:134). A Muslim takes from the repetition that holding back anger is a summary of good character.",
"virtues_022": "Abu Hurayra reports the saying in Sahih al-Bukhari 6114, in the chapter on anger. The word used for the wrestler, as-sura'a, names the man who throws every opponent, the picture of strength in his listeners' minds; the Prophet ﷺ moved that picture from the arms to the heart. The Qur'an praises those \"who, when they are angry, forgive\" (42:37) among the believers who trust their Lord. A Muslim measures strength by what he can hold back, not by whom he can knock down.",
"virtues_023": "'Abdullah ibn 'Umar narrates in Sahih al-Bukhari 24 that the Prophet ﷺ passed by a man of the Ansar admonishing his brother about his modesty, wanting him less shy, and told him to leave him alone. Al-Bukhari placed it in the Book of Faith to show that this quality belongs to belief itself. The Qur'an honours the same trait in the daughter of the old man of Madyan, who came to Musa \"walking with shyness\" (28:25). A Muslim keeps his sense of shame rather than being talked out of it.",
"virtues_024": "Abu Hurayra narrates in Sahih al-Bukhari 5997 that the Prophet ﷺ kissed his grandson al-Hasan ibn 'Ali while al-Aqra' ibn Habis sat beside him. Al-Aqra' said he had ten children and had never kissed one; the Prophet ﷺ looked at him before answering. Al-Bukhari placed the story under the chapter on mercy to children and kissing them. The Qur'an says the Prophet ﷺ was sent \"only as a mercy to the worlds\" (21:107). A Muslim learns that tenderness towards a child is part of that mercy.",
"virtues_025": "Abu Hurayra tells the story in Sahih al-Bukhari 2363, in the chapter on giving water. The traveller, just relieved of his own thirst, saw the dog licking wet earth and thought it had reached the thirst he had felt; that thought sent him back down the well. Asked whether there is a reward in animals, the Prophet ﷺ answered that there is a reward in every living creature. The Qur'an calls animals and birds \"communities like you\" (6:38). A Muslim learns that no mercy is too small to count.",
"virtues_026": "'Abdullah ibn 'Umar narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 3318, where the Prophet ﷺ says the woman entered the Fire because of the cat: she had tied it up and neither fed it nor gave it water, nor let it eat the small creatures of the earth. Al-Bukhari placed it in the Book of the Beginning of Creation, among reports about animals. The Qur'an calls every creature and bird \"communities like you\" (6:38). A Muslim takes from it that responsibility for a creature under his hand is real and weighed.",
"virtues_027": "'Aisha narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 6014, in the chapter on the recommendation concerning the neighbour. The Prophet ﷺ describes the counsel of Jibril as so constant that he expected the neighbour to receive a share of inheritance, the closest bond a family knows. The Qur'an ranks the neighbour beside kin: after parents, relatives, orphans and the needy it names \"the near neighbour, the neighbour farther away, the companion at your side\" (4:36). A Muslim treats the person next door as someone with a claim on him.",
"virtues_028": "Abu Hurayra narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 1240, placed in the Book of Funerals under the command to follow the bier. The five rights run from the smallest moment, an answered greeting or a word to someone who sneezes, to the gravest, walking behind a funeral. Each is owed simply because both people are Muslims. The Qur'an sets the same duty on a greeting: \"greet with one better than it or return it\" (4:86). A Muslim keeps these five as everyday obligations, not favours.",
"virtues_029": "'Abdullah ibn 'Amr narrates in Sahih al-Bukhari 12 that a man put the question to the Prophet ﷺ; al-Bukhari placed the answer in the Book of Faith under the heading that feeding is part of Islam. Both deeds turn outward, towards a hungry person and anyone met on the way. The Qur'an praises the righteous who \"give food, in spite of their love for it, to the needy, the orphan and the captive\" (76:8). A Muslim learns that his religion shows best in an open hand and an open greeting.",
"virtues_030": "An-Nu'man ibn Bashir narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 6011, in the chapter on mercy to people and animals. The Prophet ﷺ completes the image: when one limb complains, the rest of the body answers with sleeplessness and fever. A body does not choose to feel its hand's pain; the feeling is what being one body means. The Qur'an states the bond plainly: \"The believers are but brothers\" (49:10). A Muslim takes the trouble of another believer, near or far, as his own to feel and relieve.",
"virtues_031": "Abu Hurayra narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 6064, and it continues: do not spy, do not pry, do not envy one another, do not hate one another, do not turn your backs on one another, and be, O servants of Allah, brothers. The Qur'an gives the same order: \"Avoid much suspicion; indeed some suspicion is sin. And do not spy or backbite one another\" (49:12). Suspicion is called speech because a thought about a person soon becomes words about him. A Muslim leaves unsaid what he has not seen.",
"virtues_032": "Abu Ayyub al-Ansari narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 6077, in the chapter on shunning. The Prophet ﷺ describes the scene the rule is meant to end: two Muslims meet, and this one turns his face away and that one turns his away. The three nights are a ceiling, and the honour goes to whoever crosses the distance first with a greeting. The Qur'an, after calling the believers brothers, commands: \"make peace between your brothers\" (49:10). A Muslim learns that a quarrel has a time limit.",
"virtues_033": "Jabir ibn 'Abdullah narrates the saying in Sahih al-Bukhari 6021, and al-Bukhari made it the title of a chapter in the Book of Good Manners. The word used, ma'ruf, covers any act people recognise as good, however ordinary; the hadith gives every such act the name usually kept for money given to the poor. The Qur'an promises that \"whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it\" (99:7). A Muslim takes from it that a smile or a kind word is a charity he can give with empty pockets.",
"virtues_034": "Abu Hurayra narrates the hadith in Sahih Muslim 35, in the Book of Faith; the narration itself gives the count as seventy-odd or sixty-odd branches. The highest branch is a sentence of the tongue and the lowest a movement of the hand, a stone or a thorn lifted from where people walk, and the hadith sets both on one tree. Modesty is then named as a branch of that same tree. A Muslim learns that faith is many acts, and that clearing a path for others is one of them.",
"virtues_035": "Abu Malik al-Ash'ari narrates the hadith in Sahih Muslim 223, opening the Book of Purification. It goes on: subhan Allah and al-hamdu li-Llah together fill what lies between heaven and earth, prayer is a light, charity is a proof, patience is a brightness, and the Qur'an is an argument for you or against you. It closes: every person sets out in the morning and sells himself, freeing or ruining himself. The Qur'an says Allah \"loves those who purify themselves\" (2:222). A Muslim sees daily washing as part of his faith.",
"virtues_036": "Tamim ad-Dari narrates the hadith in Sahih Muslim 55, in the Book of Faith. The one word nasiha carries sincerity and good counsel together, wanting the good for the one it is directed to, and the list runs from Allah, His Book and His Messenger down to every ordinary Muslim. The Qur'an puts the same word in the mouth of the prophets: Hud told his people, \"I am to you a trustworthy adviser\" (7:68). A Muslim learns that his religion is measured by how sincerely he wishes good for people.",
"virtues_037": "Abu Hurayra narrates the hadith in Sahih Muslim 2564, in the Book of Righteousness. The same number gathers other sayings on the theme: a Muslim is the brother of a Muslim, neither wronging nor despising him; taqwa is here, the Prophet ﷺ pointing to his chest; and a man's blood, property and honour are inviolable to his brother. The Qur'an describes the Day when nothing benefits \"except one who comes to Allah with a sound heart\" (26:89). A Muslim works on what Allah looks at, not on what people see.",
"virtues_038": "Abu Hurayra narrates the hadith in Sahih Muslim 2699, in the Book of Remembrance and Supplication. It goes on in the same rhythm: whoever eases the burden of a person in difficulty, Allah eases things for him in this world and the next; whoever covers a Muslim's fault, Allah covers him in both; and Allah helps the servant as long as the servant helps his brother. Each reward answers the deed in kind. A Muslim learns that lifting another's trouble prepares him for his own hardest Day.",
"virtues_039": "The sentence belongs to the long hadith of Abu Hurayra in Sahih Muslim 2699, after the promises to those who help their brothers. It continues: no people gather in a house of Allah to recite His Book and study it together without tranquillity descending on them, mercy covering them, angels surrounding them and Allah mentioning them to those with Him. The Qur'an gives the Prophet ﷺ one prayer about knowledge: \"My Lord, increase me in knowledge\" (20:114). A Muslim takes every step towards a lesson as a step towards Paradise.",
"virtues_040": "'Uthman ibn 'Affan narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 5027, in the Book of the Qur'an's Virtues. The narration adds that Abu 'Abd ar-Rahman, who heard it from 'Uthman, went on teaching the Qur'an and said that this hadith had seated him in his teacher's seat. The Qur'an describes the Prophet's ﷺ own work in the same two words: reciting the verses and teaching \"them the Book and wisdom\" (62:2). A Muslim learns that the highest rank is open to anyone who learns a verse and passes it on.",
"virtues_041": "'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud narrates the hadith in Sahih Muslim 91, in the Book of Faith; it opens by warning that no one with an atom's weight of arrogance will enter Paradise. A man asked about liking fine clothes and shoes, and the Prophet ﷺ separated beauty in dress from pride in the heart. Luqman told his son the same, not to turn his cheek from people in contempt, \"for Allah does not love every self-deluded boaster\" (31:18). A Muslim checks his pride by how he receives truth and sees people.",
"virtues_042": "Ibn 'Abbas narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 6412, opening the Book of Softening the Heart. The word used, maghbun, comes from the market: it describes a man cheated in a sale, who gave away something valuable for little. Health and free time are the goods, and the loss comes from spending them on nothing. The Qur'an swears by time that \"mankind is in loss, except those who believe and do righteous deeds\" (103:2-3). A Muslim treats a healthy body and a free hour as capital to invest.",
"virtues_043": "Abu Hurayra narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 6133, and al-Bukhari made it a chapter title in the Book of Good Manners. The image is a scorpion or snake in a hole: a person bitten once knows the hole, and reaching in again is his own fault. Ya'qub shows the same caution when asked to send Binyamin with his sons: \"Should I entrust you with him except as I entrusted you with his brother before?\" (12:64). A Muslim learns from harm without bitterness, and does not repeat the same trust.",
"virtues_044": "Abu Musa al-Ash'ari narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 5534, in the chapter on musk. The other half of the comparison says that the blower of the bellows either burns your clothes or leaves you with a foul smell. Both men affect you without meaning to: the fragrance and the smoke reach whoever sits near them. The Qur'an warns that \"close friends on that Day will be enemies to one another, except the righteous\" (43:67). A Muslim chooses his company knowing that something of it will cling to him.",
"virtues_045": "'Abdullah ibn 'Umar narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 893, in the Book of the Friday Prayer. The Prophet ﷺ names a fourth shepherd after the ruler, the husband and the wife: the servant is a shepherd over his master's property and answerable for it. So the rule descends from the highest office to the smallest charge, and no one is without a flock. The Qur'an commands households likewise: \"protect yourselves and your families from a Fire\" (66:6). A Muslim knows he will be asked about whoever depends on him.",
"virtues_046": "The verse comes in Surah Fussilat after a question: who speaks better than one who calls to Allah and says \"I am of the Muslims\" (41:33). It opens by stating that \"the good deed and the bad deed are not equal\" (41:34), then gives the instruction. The next verse says this is granted only to the patient (41:35), and the one after tells him to seek refuge in Allah from Satan's whisper (41:36). A Muslim learns that answering harm with good needs patience, and can make an enemy a friend.",
"virtues_047": "The verse opens the portrait of the servants of the Most Merciful closing Surah Al-Furqan. After the humble walk and the word of peace come the other marks: nights spent before their Lord prostrating and standing (25:64), spending neither wastefully nor meanly (25:67), passing by idle talk with dignity (25:72). The passage ends with their reward, the high chamber of Paradise granted for patience, where they are met with peace (25:75). A Muslim reading 25:63 learns that the peace he gives on earth is the peace that greets him there.",
"virtues_048": "The command sits in a series of instructions in Surah Al-Isra that begins with worshipping Allah alone and honouring parents (17:23). Verse 17:34 opens with the orphan, \"Do not approach the orphan's property except in the way that is best until he reaches maturity\", and then turns to the covenant. The next verses order full measure and a straight balance (17:35) and warn that hearing, sight and heart will be questioned (17:36). A Muslim learns that a promise, like a weight in the market, is something he will account for.",
"virtues_049": "The verse follows a command in Surah Al-Isra to give the relative, the needy and the traveller their right, \"and do not spend wastefully\" (17:26). Verse 17:27 then explains why: waste imitates the devils, whose leader was most ungrateful for what his Lord gave him. Two verses later the balance is set on both sides: \"Do not make your hand chained to your neck, nor extend it completely\" (17:29). A Muslim learns that the wrong is not in giving but in throwing away, and that ingratitude is at its root.",
"virtues_050": "Abu Hurayra narrates the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 6406, in the Book of Invocations, under the chapter on glorifying Allah. The two phrases join praise to glorification: the first declares Allah free of every fault and thanks Him, the second calls Him Immense. The Qur'an describes the scale: Allah sets up the just scales on the Day of Resurrection, and \"if there is the weight of a mustard seed, We will bring it forth\" (21:47). A Muslim keeps these words on his tongue, knowing how much they weigh.",
}
